<Base title="Method">
  <h1>Method</h1>
  <p class="lede">Every claim on this site carries a confidence and a source. The point of saying so is
  that it lets a claim be overturned.</p>

  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Label</th><th>Means</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><span class="chip doc">documented</span></td>
        <td>A named source with a reference, and where possible the scan itself.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="chip">inferred</span></td>
        <td>A reasoned conclusion from documented facts, with the reasoning written out so it can be argued with.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="chip dis">disputed</span></td>
        <td>Asserted somewhere in the family record, but not supported — and sometimes contradicted — by what can be seen.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="chip">family lore</span></td>
        <td>Told and remembered, not corroborated. Kept because it is precious; labelled because pretending
        otherwise is how family myths become family history.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>

  <h2>Agostino Falcone, and what replaced him</h2>
  <p>The line was previously carried above Pasquale Falco to an <strong>Agostino Falcone</strong> born
  about 1740, with a wife <strong>Elisabetta Sabina</strong>. That link has now been not merely doubted
  but <strong>superseded</strong>. Pasquale's own baptism has been found.</p>
  <blockquote>Anno Domini millesimo septingentesimo sexagesimo septimo, die vero vigesima quarta …
  baptizavi infantem natum ex <strong>Mattheo Falco</strong>, ed <strong>Francisca Crisci</strong>
  coniugibus huius archipresbyteralis collegiatae ecclesiae Sanctae Andreae Apostoli Terrae Argenti,
  qui nominatus est <strong>Paschalis</strong>.
  <span class="small">— Arienzo baptisms, 24 October 1767</span></blockquote>
  <p>The father is <strong>Matteo Falco</strong>, not Agostino Falcone. The reasons the Falcone claim was
  doubted before the baptism turned up are worth keeping, because they are the reasons it should have
  been doubted:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The surname was different</strong> — <em>Falcone</em>, not <em>Falco</em>.</li>
    <li><strong>Falcone is not an Arienzo surname.</strong> Across 361 register pages mentioning a Falco,
    <em>Falcone</em> appeared on two, both index lists. <em>Crisci</em> appeared on 113,
    <em>Morgillo</em> on 76.</li>
    <li><strong>The one Falcone in a register entry is the officiating priest</strong>, not a parent.</li>
    <li><strong>No record was ever produced</strong> for him or his wife.</li>
    <li><strong>FamilySearch's index conflates the two names</strong>, returning both for either query —
    verified: identical result counts across four separate collections. A researcher can acquire a
    Falcone ancestor from that index without ever choosing to.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Matteo Falco is now the head of the documented line. Above him the baptism register has a gap
  between 1738 and 1761, and his own baptism most likely falls in it.</p>

  <h2>On dates read by machine</h2>
  <p>Much of what is new here was found by handwriting recognition applied to registers no one has indexed
  by hand. It is a remarkable tool and it is wrong about digits more often than about names. Where a
  machine-read date is load-bearing it is flagged, and it should be confirmed against the image before it
  is believed. One such case is on the <a href="./arienzo/">Arienzo</a> page.</p>

  <h2>Four things the Arienzo registers taught us to distrust</h2>
  <p>Reading twenty annual volumes act by act turns up habits of the source that are easy to miss from a
  single record, and each of them qualifies claims this archive itself has made.</p>

  <h3>A declarant's street is not where he lived</h3>
  <p>On <strong>20 June 1823</strong> Giuseppe Falco declares two deaths on the same day. In one he is
  «domiciliato… <strong>strada Camellara</strong>», in the other «domiciliato… <strong>strada
  Sant'Andrea</strong>». He had not moved between breakfast and supper. In this printed form the
  declarant described as <em>vicino</em> — neighbour — is written at <strong>the street of the house
  where the death happened</strong>. So a declarant's street records where the dead person lived, not
  where the declarant did. Anywhere this site places a person on a street from a declarant line alone,
  that is the weaker kind of evidence.</p>

  <h3>Ages move by a year a day</h3>
  <p>The same Giuseppe Falco is <strong>forty-three</strong> in one of those two acts and
  <strong>forty-two</strong> in the other — same clerk, same afternoon. Across nineteen months his stated
  age implies birth years from 1780 to 1784. Ages in these registers are an approximation offered by a
  neighbour, and this archive treats a single stated age as a range, never a date.</p>

  <h3>Forenames repeat until they are useless alone</h3>
  <p>Arienzo held at least <strong>three Carlantonio Morgillo</strong>, <strong>three Maria Falco</strong>,
  two Paolo Rivetti, two Marcello Rivetti, two Nicola Morgillo, two Francesco Quotolo and two Arcangelo
  Crisci — all alive or dying within a decade of one another. Identity here rests on the
  <em>combination</em> of parents, spouse, street and trade. A matching forename is where an
  identification starts, never where it ends.</p>

  <h3>Two surnames can be one letter apart, and the clerk knew the difference</h3>
  <p>From 1823 this archive recorded a household as <strong>«Francesco Cossi and Maria Falco» of strada
  Camellara</strong> — the house whose five dead children Giuseppe Falco declared, and the strongest
  Falco kinship lead outside the direct line. <strong>It is CIOFFI.</strong> Read at full resolution,
  the 1825 volume writes the Camellara family with a dotted <em>i</em> after the C — <em>Cesare
  Cioffi</em>, <em>Francesco Cioffi</em>, <em>Giuseppa Cioffi</em> — and, in the same hand and the same
  book, writes the unrelated family of <strong>strada Cossi</strong> without one: <em>Domenico Cossi</em>,
  <em>Crescenzo Cossi</em>. The clerk distinguished two families; this archive conflated them for five
  years across six annual registers.</p>
  <p>The correction is <strong>published rather than swapped</strong>. The reading logs keep the original
  text and carry a banner saying what was wrong; the plate showing the four words side by side is on the
  <a href={u("/documents")}>Documents</a> page. Two lessons follow. The first is procedural: a surname
  read at strip resolution is a hypothesis, and only a full-resolution reading of a <em>distinguishing
  pair</em> — the same clerk writing both candidates — settles it. The second is that the same doubt is
  still open elsewhere — or was. The priest buried in 1827 as <em>Don Giuseppe Cr-i-sci or C-o-ssi,
  son of Andrea, possidente, and Orsola Zimbardo</em> was settled sixteen years later by an act
  nobody was looking for: in June 1843 the register buries <strong>Celia Cioffi</strong>, eighty,
  <em>«figlia di ANDREA CIOFFI defunto, di professione POSSIDENTE … e di ORSOLA ZIMBARDO defunta»</em>.
  Same father, same trade, same mother, spelled plainly. <strong>He was Don Giuseppe Cioffi.</strong>
  Which is the third lesson: a hand that will not resolve is sometimes resolved by a different clerk
  in a different decade, and the way to catch that is to read whole volumes rather than hunt single
  acts. One reading is still open — the household held as <em>Marcello Crisci &amp; Maria Rosa
  Mauro</em>, which 1828 writes as <em>Marcello Cossi</em> with the identical wife.</p>

  <h2>What is not known</h2>
  <p>An archive that only publishes its findings is half a record. Everything this one has failed to
  establish — and every search that came back empty — is set out at
  <a href="../open-questions/">Open questions</a>, including the ground already covered, so that the
  next attempt does not repeat it.</p>

  <h2>Living people</h2>
  <p>Living people are <strong>omitted from this build entirely</strong> — not hidden, not gated, not present
  in the output. A removal request is honoured within days, without argument and without requiring a reason.</p>
</Base>
